Package: shapley
Type: Package
Title: Weighted Mean SHAP and CI for Robust Feature Assessment in ML Grid
Version: 0.8.0
Authors@R:
    person("E. F. Haghish",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           email = "haghish@hotmail.com")
Depends:
    R (>= 4.1.0)
Description: This R package introduces Weighted Mean SHapley Additive exPlanations
  (WMSHAP), a framework for estimating and combining SHAP values across a
  grid of fine-tuned machine learning models or the base learners of stacked
  ensembles. Rather than relying on a single best-performing model, WMSHAP
  combines SHAP values from multiple models, weighting their contributions
  according to predictive performance. Model weights can be calculated using
  R-squared for regression and classification models or, for binary
  classifiers, the area under the precision-recall curve (AUCPR), the area
  under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC), or the F2 score. 
  The package also provides weighted confidence intervals for mean SHAP
  values, allowing feature importance to be evaluated across multiple models
  while accounting for variability in their contributions. This approach
  particularly improves accuracy if feature importance assessment at both local
  (individual) and global (feature) levels and improves the stability of feature 
  importance rankins (see the GitHub repository for references and details).  
  By integrating SHAP values across models, WMSHAP reduces reliance on any single 
  potentially overfitted or biased model, providing a more comprehensive and 
  generalizable assessment of feature importance. The package further implements 
  hypothesis tests to assess the statistical significance of individual feature 
  contributions, as well as comparative tests to evaluate differences in SHAP 
  contributions between features. It also provides criteria for automatic feature 
  selection across model grids and stacked ensembles, eliminating the need to 
  specify an arbitrary number of top features. This approach supports a more
  representative assessment of feature importance by considering evidence
  from multiple models rather than relying exclusively on the best-performing
  model. The package also provides visualizations of SHAP values at individual, 
  feature, and factor levels, including feature-selection plots based on weighted 
  mean WMSHAP ratios. Supported SHAP backends include 'h2o', 'treeshap', 'shapr', 
  and 'kernelshap', as well as native 'gpboost' explanations of the boosted-tree 
  component. 
